Rejected at Step 0; no generator was manufactured.

The full paper shows that:

- The smallest admissible native graph has 310 vertices, exceeding the 256-atom answer cap for a literal Hamilton cycle.
- A compressed ten-orbit lift certificate is exactly verifiable, but the paper’s lifting method constructs it in at most 22 operations—the compact route costs the same.
- An expanded representation requires thousands of inspections even after recognizing the structure, violating the 300-operation cap.
- The proposed arbitrary planted-cycle family is not licensed by the paper’s `PSL(2,k)` orbital-graph regime and has no distributional hardness basis.

The complete evidence, mechanical-versus-compact costs, theorem references, and candidate audit are in [REJECTED.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2411.17780/REJECTED.md). The decision is based on the paper’s Section 2 lifting lemma and Section 3 orbital construction. [Full arXiv paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2411.17780v1)
